{% extends "base.peb" %}
{% block title %}Authorized Product List{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
    <h1>California WIC Authorized Product List</h1>
    <p class="lead">Source: <a href="https://data.chhs.ca.gov/dataset/wic-authorized-product-list">data.chhs.ca.gov/dataset/wic-authorized-product-list</a>
    </p>

    <h2>About the dataset</h2>
    <p>This dataset represents the list of CA WIC authorized food items identified by food category and subcategory.
        Each item is uniquely identified by a Universal Product Code (UPC) or Price Look-Up code (PLU) for WIC
        electronic benefit transfer (EBT). The WIC Authorized vendors use the CA Authorized Product List (APL) to
        transact WIC food items at cash registers. The APL plays a crucial role in supporting WIC EBT purchases. WIC EBT
        requires vendor systems to maintain the APL to match the scanned food items' UPCs to ensure they are on the APL.
        The food items identified by UPC and PLU can be found in the data files below. When you download the files,
        Excel may prompt you to automatically format the data. If prompted, you may want to hit ‘Don’t Convert’ so that
        Excel leaves the data as is without any formatting or data conversions.</p>
    <p>The Women, Infants and Children (WIC) Program is a federally-funded health and nutrition program that provides
        assistance to pregnant women, new mothers, infants, and children under age five. WIC helps California families
        by providing food benefits to individual participants based on their nutritional need and risk assessment. The
        food benefits can be used to purchase healthy supplemental foods from approximately 3,800 WIC authorized vendor
        stores throughout the State. WIC also provides nutritional education, breastfeeding support, healthcare
        referrals, and other community services. Participants must meet income guidelines and other criteria. Currently,
        84 WIC agencies provide services monthly to approximately one million participants at several hundred sites in
        local communities throughout the State.</p>

    <h2>Published files</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>File</th>
            <th>Rows</th>
            <th>Contents</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>california-wic-authorized-product-list-upc.csv</code></td>
            <td>6,808</td>
            <td>Packaged foods identified by UPC.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>california-wic-authorized-product-list-plu.csv</code></td>
            <td>2,585</td>
            <td>Produce identified by PLU.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>*-data-dictionary.csv</code></td>
            <td></td>
            <td>Column lists for the two files.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>datapackage.json</code></td>
            <td></td>
            <td>Dataset metadata.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How it is stored in our database</h2>
    <p>Loaded by <code>db/changelog/seed/02-authorized-products.yaml</code>. The UPC and PLU files have the same
        columns, apart from the fruit and vegetable type that only the UPC file has, so both go into one table with a
        <code>code_type</code> column.</p>
    <h3><code>authorized_product</code></h3>
    <p>9,393 rows. <code>(code_type, code)</code> is unique, and <code>category_code</code> is indexed.</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Column</th>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Published column</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>id</code></td>
            <td>bigint</td>
            <td>Generated key.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>code_type</code></td>
            <td>varchar(3)</td>
            <td><code>UPC</code> or <code>PLU</code>, from the source file.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>code</code></td>
            <td>varchar(20)</td>
            <td>UPC or PLU. Stored as text so the 14-digit UPCs keep their leading zeros, as in
                <code>00046567002071</code>.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>category_code</code></td>
            <td>varchar(4)</td>
            <td>CATEGORY, such as <code>19</code>. Published padded with spaces.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>category_description</code></td>
            <td>varchar(100)</td>
            <td>CATEGORY_DESCRIPTION</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>subcategory_code</code></td>
            <td>varchar(4)</td>
            <td>SUBCATEGORY, such as <code>001</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>subcategory_description</code></td>
            <td>varchar(100)</td>
            <td>SUBCATEGORY_DESCRIPTION</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>description</code></td>
            <td>varchar(200)</td>
            <td>PRODUCT_DESCRIPTION_AND_SIZE, abbreviated as on a receipt, such as <code>BstY LGBrwn Rice-16OZ</code>.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>unit_of_measure</code></td>
            <td>varchar(10)</td>
            <td>UOM, such as <code>OZ</code>, <code>GAL</code> or <code>CTR</code>. <code>$$$</code> marks cash value
                benefit items.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>fruit_vegetable_type</code></td>
            <td>varchar(50)</td>
            <td>FRUIT_AND_VEGETABLE_TYPE, such as <code>Canned Fruits and Vegetables</code>. UPC file only;
                <code>NULL</code> for PLUs and most non-produce UPCs.
            </td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">Codes are stored as text, never as numbers, for the same reason the description warns about Excel:
        converting them drops the leading zeros.</p>

    <h2>API</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Request</th>
            <th>Returns</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>GET /api/products?q=</code></td>
            <td>Pages of products ordered by description. Every word in <code>q</code> must appear in the code,
                description, category, subcategory or fruit and vegetable type. Also takes <code>type</code>
                (<code>UPC</code> or <code>PLU</code>), <code>category</code>, <code>page</code> and <code>size</code>.
                The "Which foods qualify?" page calls this.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>GET /api/products/{code}</code></td>
            <td>The products with that exact code, or 404.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>GET /api/products/categories</code></td>
            <td>Each category with its number of products.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <pre><code>GET /api/products?q=brown%20rice</code></pre>
    <pre><code>SELECT code_type, code, description, subcategory_description
FROM authorized_product
WHERE lower(description || ' ' || subcategory_description) LIKE '%brown%'
  AND lower(description || ' ' || subcategory_description) LIKE '%rice%'
ORDER BY description;</code></pre>
{% endblock %}
